//
// QWEN2.5-3B Q4 prefill TTFT vs padded prefill length, into
// qwen25_3b_q4.prefill_sweep.json (collected by the nightly LLM benchmark).
//
// The other axis from run_npu2_sweep.lit: that one walks KV depth for decode
// tok/s, this one walks the length the prefill ELFs are BUILT for.
//
// This model already had the decode sweep and a working `profile-prefill`
// target; only the lit was missing. Without it the model kept a single-point
// row in the scalar table, and that point is the profile prompt -- 5 tokens.
// A TTFT measured at 5 tokens sitting in the same section as curves measured at
// 512-4096 invites reading them as comparable, which they are not.
//
// This Makefile names BOTH knobs differently from every other model, so both
// have to be redirected: SEQ_LEN for the engine length (--seq-env) and BENCH_L
// for how many ids the bench feeds (--bench-env). sweep_prefill.py passes CTX=
// on the make line regardless, which this Makefile ignores.
//
// Leaving --bench-env off does not corrupt the published TTFT -- the prefill
// pads to the engine length, so feeding 2048 ids to a 4096 engine costs the
// same 4096-engine time (measured: 10312 ms without it, 10274 ms with, 0.4%).
// It is still wrong to leave off, for two reasons. The driver's own
// "[bench] L=..." line then reports the wrong length and a tok/s off by the
// ratio (L=2048, 199 tok/s for what is a 4096 point at 399). And BENCH_L stays
// pinned at its 2048 default, so the day the stitcher below is fixed, the 512
// and 1024 points would stop failing at build and start failing on the
// driver's `assert N <= self.seq` instead -- a fresh failure for an unrelated
// reason, at the exact moment the real one was fixed.
//
// 512 and 1024 are expected-fail with a named status: stitcher_mixed_tile_m.
// This model runs the bf16 qwen25_3b prefill (only its decode is q4), so it
// inherits that example's limitation -- the registry's best method for the
// square Q/O proj flips from fused-cast (tile_m=64) at 2048 to drain
// (tile_m=32) at 512/1024, and the fused stitcher assumes one tile_m across the
// GEMMs it merges. Not a registry problem; both rows are measured and correct.
//
// Latency only, never a correctness gate (run_npu2_verify.lit is that). The
// per-length first-token argmax the driver prints is recorded with each point;
// both measured lengths passed when this file was written.
//
// Measured on a Ryzen AI 9 HX 370: 4807 ms at 2048, 9695 ms at 4096.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano, llvm_link_pre23, hf_token, hfweights_qwen_qwen2_5_3b_instruct, hfweights_fastflowlm_qwen2_5_3b_instruct_npu2
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_peano_prefill_sweep
// RUN: cd test_peano_prefill_sweep
// RUN: %PYTHON %S/../bench/sweep_prefill.py --model-name qwen25_3b_q4 --makefile %S/Makefile --lengths 512,1024,2048,4096 --expect-fail 512,1024 --seq-env SEQ_LEN --bench-env BENCH_L --peano-dir %PEANO_INSTALL_DIR --out qwen25_3b_q4.prefill_sweep.json
// RUN: FileCheck %s < qwen25_3b_q4.prefill_sweep.json
// CHECK: "model": "qwen25_3b_q4"
// CHECK: "axis": "prefill_len"
// A parsed number at the reference length, not just a well-formed file: if the
// driver's TTFT line drifts out of sweep_prefill.py's regex every point goes
// null and the published curve silently empties while the test stays green.
// CHECK: "prefill_len": 2048
// CHECK-NEXT: "ttft_ms": {{[0-9]}}
